<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.Ratatoskr</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.Ratatoskr</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="11.0.0" />

    <!--
      OMEMO (XEP-0384) needs X25519 and Ed25519, and curve arithmetic by
      hand is the one place where an error does not come out and costs
      everything.

      .NET 11 brings half of it. X25519DiffieHellman is new in
      System.Security.Cryptography: in the 10.0.15 reference assembly the
      string "X25519" does not appear a single time, in the 11.0.0 one it
      names a class. Ed25519 is in neither - "WithEd25519" appears, but that
      is part of a composite ML-DSA algorithm name and not a key anything can
      be signed with.

      So the package stays, and the half the BCL now covers stays with it:
      one protocol served by two implementations of the same curve buys
      nothing but a second set of edge cases, at the one place where an edge
      case is silent.

      Listed expressly although Hermod brings it along already: whoever uses
      a transitive dependency directly loses it at the moment at which the
      previous owner puts it down.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.7.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.Ratatoskr.Tests" />

    <!--
      The second name is back, and it is not the stopgap D97 removed. That one
      named an assembly for a move that had not happened; this one names the
      suite that holds the interoperability tests since E19 - the checks
      against Prosody, ejabberd and python-omemo, which live where their setups
      live and not here.

      What it is needed for is one member: XMPPServer.OnInstanceCreated, the
      internal event the guard from D54 hangs itself on. Without this line the
      moved tests would run without that guard, and a programming error in the
      server would land in Hermod's log again instead of failing the test -
      exactly the state D18, D19 and D54 took three steps to leave.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="org.GraphDefined.Vanaheimr.Ratatoskr.XMPPConformanceTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Hermod\Hermod\Hermod.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
